  <result aggregation-status="0" type="2">
   <title>
    <narrative xml:lang="EN">Output 1.1. Increased institutional capacity of employer and business membership organizations (EBMOs)</narrative>
   </title>
   <description>
    <narrative xml:lang="EN">In August 2022, the Employers Association of South Sudan (EASS) launched its strategic plan, covering the period from 2022 to 2025, with a primary focus on re-establishing the organization&#x2019;s board and secretariat, expanding its membership, and forging alliances and partnerships. The launch event, attended by 100 participants from the national business community, high-level government officials including the Minister of Labour, and trade union representatives including the President of the apex workers&#x2019; organization, not only heightened EASS&#x2019;s visibility but served as an opportunity to affirm its position as the voice of businesses in South Sudan. 
As part of the strategic plan&#x2019;s implementation, EASS conducted a mapping of potential members in November 2022 with a view to developing new services and attracting new members. In addition, a mapping of donors active in South Sudan was undertaken with a view to mobilizing resources for the implementation of the strategic plan. The mobilization of adequate financial resources is critical for implementing EASS&#x2019;s new strategic plan, start providing services to members including effective representation and strengthen the secretariat. Capitalizing on its newfound visibility and credibility linked to the launching of the strategic implementation, EASS developed its first communication strategy in September 2023.  This strategy integrates digital communication tools, with a new website serving as the primary platform for reaching out to potential members and donors, and for advocating policy changes. Furthermore, externally EASS strengthened its position and standing, by taking over the presidency of the Confederation of the Intergovernmental Authority on Development (IGAD) Employers in September 2022. The ILO supported EASS to strengthen its position and visibility through the provision of technical support. Between March and August 2022, the ILO supported EASS prepare the launching event for its strategic plan and ensuring that EASS&#x2019;s capacity was built to position themselves as South Sudan&#x2019;s employer constituent. Between September 2022 and February 2023, the ILO built the capacities of six of EASS board and secretariat by conducting training on how to effectively govern and manage an employers&#x2019; organization.  Furthermore, the ILO supported them to undertake a membership analysis through surveys and in-depth-interviews with members and potential members creating the basis for EASS to increase their membership. Between July and November 2023, the ILO helped EASS develop a communication strategy and their website to allow better and more regular communication with their members as well as attracting new members.</narrative>
   </description>
